@id     06_lists
@title  Linked lists
@topic  lists
@lang   c
@show   heap

@theory
A linked list is a chain of boxes called nodes. Each node holds a value and the
address of the next node. The last node holds NULL: there is no next node.

    struct node {
        int value;
        struct node *next;   // address of the next node, or NULL
    };

Nodes are reserved with malloc, so the list can grow while the program runs.
head points to the first node. To visit every node, start at head and follow next
until you reach NULL:

    for (struct node *p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next)
        printf("%d ", p->value);

p->value means "the value field of the node that p points to".
Every node reserved with malloc must be given back with free.

Your task: the program reads n (1..100) and then n integers. Each number is added
to the front of the list, so the list holds them in reverse order. Print the values
of the list from head to the end, separated by spaces.

Input:   3
         1 2 3
Output:  3 2 1

@read
## A chain of boxes
An array has a fixed size. A linked list can grow while the program runs. It is a chain of nodes. Each node holds a value and the address of the next node.

    struct node {
        int value;
        struct node *next;   // the next node, or NULL at the end
    };

## Making a node
Nodes are reserved with malloc while the program runs. `->` reaches a field through a pointer: `p->value` means "the value field of the node p points to".

    struct node *head = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
    head->value = 10;
    head->next = NULL;       // the only node, so nothing comes after it

## Walking the list
Start at head and follow next until you reach NULL:

    for (struct node *p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next) {
        printf("%d ", p->value);
    }

The last node's next is NULL, which is how the loop knows the list has ended.

## Giving memory back
Every node reserved with malloc must be freed with free when you are done. Save the next node first, because after free the node may not be used:

    while (head != NULL) {
        struct node *after = head->next;
        free(head);
        head = after;
    }

## Read more
Beej's Guide to C Programming, chapter 8 "Structs" and chapter 12 "Manual Memory Allocation" — free at beej.us/guide/bgc

@starter
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct node {
    int value;
    struct node *next;
};

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);

    struct node *head = NULL;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        struct node *fresh = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
        scanf("%d", &fresh->value);
        fresh->next = head;
        head = fresh;
    }

    // print every value from head to the end

    printf("\n");
    while (head != NULL) {
        struct node *after = head->next;
        free(head);
        head = after;
    }
    return 0;
}

@solution
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct node {
    int value;
    struct node *next;
};

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);

    struct node *head = NULL;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        struct node *fresh = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
        scanf("%d", &fresh->value);
        fresh->next = head;
        head = fresh;
    }

    for (struct node *p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next) {
        printf("%d ", p->value);
    }

    printf("\n");
    while (head != NULL) {
        struct node *after = head->next;
        free(head);
        head = after;
    }
    return 0;
}

@test
@input
3
1 2 3
@expected
3 2 1

@test
@input
1
42
@expected
42

@hidden_test
@input
5
-1 0 7 7 10
@expected
10 7 7 0 -1

#! ------------------------------------------------------------------
#! Steps: Explore -> Understand -> Practice. The task above is the Practice step.
#! ------------------------------------------------------------------

@step explore
@title  Reserve a box
@scene  heap
@text
Press malloc, then free(p), and watch the reserved boxes appear and disappear.
malloc reserves memory while the program runs. Every node of a linked list is reserved this way.

@step watch
@title  Watch a list being built
@code
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct node {
    int value;
    struct node *next;
};

int main(void) {
    struct node *third = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
    third->value = 30;
    third->next = NULL;

    struct node *second = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
    second->value = 20;
    second->next = third;

    struct node *head = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
    head->value = 10;
    head->next = second;

    for (struct node *p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next) {
        printf("%d ", p->value);
    }
    printf("\n");

    free(head);
    free(second);
    free(third);
    return 0;
}
@text
Press Next line and watch three nodes appear and link up.
Each node holds a value and the address of the next node. The last one holds NULL. Then the loop follows next from node to node.

@step predict
@title  Follow two arrows
@code
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct node {
    int value;
    struct node *next;
};

int main(void) {
    struct node *b = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
    b->value = 7;
    b->next = NULL;
    struct node *a = malloc(sizeof(struct node));
    a->value = 4;
    a->next = b;
    printf("%d\n", a->next->value);
    free(a);
    free(b);
    return 0;
}
@line   16
@expr   a->next->value
@text
What is a->next->value after line 16 runs?
a->next is the node that a points to next. Then take its value.

@step choose
@title  The end of the list
@code
for (struct node *p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next)
@text
How does this loop know that it has reached the end of the list?
@option The last node's next is NULL
@option The list has 10 nodes
@option p becomes 0 after printing
@option malloc returns NULL
@correct 1
@answer
The last node holds NULL in next. When p becomes NULL, p != NULL is false and the loop stops.

@step order
@title  Put the walk in order
@text
Put the lines in order so the program prints every value of the list.
Start at head, print the value, then move to the next node.
@code
for (struct node *p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next) {
    printf("%d ", p->value);
}
printf("\n");

@step find_bug
@title  Find the bug
@text
Fix the loop so it also prints the last node.
Right now it stops one node early: it prints 1 2 instead of 1 2 3.
@starter
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct node {
    int value;
    struct node *next;
};

int main(void) {
    struct node nodes[3] = {{1, NULL}, {2, NULL}, {3, NULL}};
    nodes[0].next = &nodes[1];
    nodes[1].next = &nodes[2];
    struct node *head = &nodes[0];

    struct node *p = head;
    while (p->next != NULL) {
        printf("%d ", p->value);
        p = p->next;
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
@editable 16-16
@solution
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct node {
    int value;
    struct node *next;
};

int main(void) {
    struct node nodes[3] = {{1, NULL}, {2, NULL}, {3, NULL}};
    nodes[0].next = &nodes[1];
    nodes[1].next = &nodes[2];
    struct node *head = &nodes[0];

    struct node *p = head;
    while (p != NULL) {
        printf("%d ", p->value);
        p = p->next;
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
@test
@expected
1 2 3

@step explain
@title  Explain it
@text
Why does every linked list end with a node whose next is NULL?
@rubric
NULL; end; last; stop
@answer
NULL means "no next node". The loop that walks the list stops when it reaches NULL,
so without it the program would follow an address that does not belong to the list.

@step practice
@title  Practice
